window.BOOK_FC=[{"t":"Types of data and scales","s":13,"e":17,"c":[{"q":"Name the four scales of measurement in order, and the property each adds.","a":"Nominal (names), ordinal (adds order), interval (adds equal gaps), ratio (adds a true zero). Each level keeps every property of the one before.","p":13},{"q":"Why is full-scale IQ an interval rather than a ratio scale?","a":"Its steps are equal (15 points is one SD), but zero is arbitrary: IQ 0 does not mean no intelligence. So IQ 120 is not 'twice as clever' as 60. Celsius temperature behaves the same way.","p":14},{"q":"Core temperature in °C is recorded in suspected neuroleptic malignant syndrome. What type of data is it, and what would change that?","a":"Interval: equal steps, but zero is the arbitrary freezing point of water. Measured in kelvin, which has a true zero, it would be a ratio scale.","p":17},{"q":"CGI-Severity is recorded as whole numbers from 1 to 7. Why is it not a discrete numerical variable?","a":"It is an ordered category: the step from 1 to 2 is not shown to equal the step from 6 to 7, so it is ordinal. A count such as number of admissions, with a true zero, is discrete numerical.","p":15},{"q":"Which summary measure suits nominal, ordinal and interval or ratio data respectively?","a":"Nominal: mode and percentages. Ordinal: median and IQR, with non-parametric tests. Interval or ratio: mean and SD; ratio data can also use the geometric mean.","p":14},{"q":"Can PHQ-9 or PANSS totals be analysed with means and t-tests?","a":"Strictly they are ordinal, as nobody has shown each point is equal. In practice they are often treated as interval when there are many points and the distribution is roughly normal, but you should say so.","p":14},{"q":"Of 120 patients, 36 relapse. Give the proportion and the odds of relapse.","a":"Proportion 0.30; odds 0.43. A proportion keeps the numerator inside the denominator (36 of 120); odds compare events with non-events (36 against 84).","p":15,"f":"Proportion = 36 / 120 = 0.30; odds = 36 / 84 = 0.43"},{"q":"Twelve patients were followed for 97 person-months in total, and 4 repeated self-harm. What is the incidence rate per 100 person-years?","a":"About 49 per 100 person-years. 97 months is 8.08 person-years. The 33% (4 of 12) is a proportion, which ignores how long each person was actually at risk.","p":16,"f":"Rate = 4 / (97 / 12) = 4 / 8.08 = 0.49 per person-year"},{"q":"A report calls point prevalence of depression a 'rate'. Why is this incorrect?","a":"Prevalence is a proportion with no time unit. A true rate has person-time in the denominator, counting how long each person was at risk.","p":15},{"q":"Convert a probability of relapse of 0.2 into odds.","a":"0.25 (1 to 4). Odds are the chance of the event over the chance of no event; when events are rare, odds and probability are close, but they diverge as probability rises.","p":17,"f":"Odds = p / (1 − p) = 0.2 / 0.8 = 0.25"}]},{"t":"Samples, populations and sampling","s":18,"e":23,"c":[{"q":"What defines a probability sample, and what does it allow that a non-probability sample does not?","a":"Everyone in the sampling frame has a known, non-zero chance of selection. That lets sampling error be quantified. Convenience, quota, purposive and snowball samples do not allow this.","p":18},{"q":"Researchers randomly pick 4 of 16 community teams and screen every patient on those caseloads. Which sampling method is this?","a":"Cluster sampling: the random unit is the group, and everyone in each chosen group is included. Randomly sampling patients within the chosen teams would make it multistage.","p":19},{"q":"Distinguish stratified random sampling from cluster sampling.","a":"Stratified samples randomly from every subgroup, guaranteeing representation and improving precision. Cluster takes all members of some groups and skips the rest: cheaper, but less precise, and it can miss whole subgroups.","p":19},{"q":"Clusters of 15 patients have an intracluster correlation of 0.05. What is the design effect, and the effective size of a 60-person cluster sample?","a":"Design effect 1.7, so 60 clustered people carry the information of about 35 independent ones. People in one team resemble each other, so each adds less new information.","p":19,"f":"Design effect = 1 + (m − 1) × ICC = 1 + 14 × 0.05 = 1.7; effective n = 60 / 1.7 ≈ 35"},{"q":"Interviewers fill a set number of men and women in each age band with whoever they meet first. Why is this not stratified sampling?","a":"It is quota sampling. Both fill subgroups, but quota sampling picks people non-randomly, so it is a non-probability method and sampling error cannot be measured.","p":21},{"q":"A survey's sample size rises from 100 to 400. By how much does the standard error of the prevalence estimate change?","a":"It halves. Precision improves with the square root of n, so quadrupling n divides SE by 2. 'A quarter' is the trap.","p":21,"f":"SE = √(p(1 − p) / n); 400 / 100 = 4, √4 = 2"},{"q":"A survey of 400 carers from a support group estimates depression prevalence at 34% (true value 24%). Would sampling 4,000 such carers fix this?","a":"No. Size shrinks random error only; a biased frame stays biased. A bigger sample would give a more precise estimate of the wrong value.","p":21},{"q":"Of 600 people invited, 372 are assessed; 301 are followed up at 12 months. What is the response rate, and what is 301 / 372?","a":"Response rate 62%; 301 / 372 (81%) is retention at 12 months. If non-responders differ, for example being more unwell, non-response bias follows.","p":22,"f":"Response rate = 372 / 600 = 0.62"},{"q":"600 people are drawn at random from a register of 1,720. What are the register and the 35% called?","a":"The register is the sampling frame, the list actually sampled; anyone missing from it can never be chosen. The 35% is the sampling fraction: sample size over frame size.","p":23,"f":"Sampling fraction = 600 / 1,720 = 0.35"},{"q":"Which sampling methods suit a hidden group, qualitative work and a diagnostic accuracy study respectively?","a":"Snowball for hidden groups with no list; purposive for qualitative work; consecutive for diagnostic studies. None is random: random means a chance mechanism chose the people.","p":18}]},{"t":"Mean, median and mode","s":24,"e":28,"c":[{"q":"Ward length of stay has a mode of 14, median of 23 and mean of 28.4 days. Which best describes a typical stay, and why?","a":"The median, 23 days. The data are positively skewed, and a few very long stays drag the mean up. The median depends only on order, so it is robust to outliers.","p":25},{"q":"In positively and negatively skewed data, how are mean, median and mode ordered?","a":"Positive skew: mode < median < mean. Negative skew: mean < median < mode. The mean always runs furthest into the long tail.","p":25},{"q":"MMSE scores in the community cluster near the maximum of 30 with a long tail of low scores. What is the skew called?","a":"Negative (left) skew, here a ceiling effect. Skew is named after the tail, not the peak, so the mean sits below the median, which sits below the mode.","p":25},{"q":"If the median is the 'typical' stay, why might a finance team still want the mean length of stay?","a":"Total bed-days = mean × number of admissions. The median answers 'what is typical?'; the mean answers 'how many bed-days in total?'","p":25,"f":"Total bed-days = mean × n"},{"q":"Eighteen referral ages are sorted; the 9th is 23 and the 10th is 24. What is the median?","a":"23.5. With an even number of values, the median is the average of the middle two. Forgetting to sort first, or taking just one middle value, are classic errors.","p":26,"f":"Median = (23 + 24) / 2 = 23.5"},{"q":"A stem-and-leaf plot has stems 1 to 5 with 5, 7, 3, 2 and 1 leaves. How many people are in the sample?","a":"18. Each leaf is one person, and the stems are not people. Sum the leaves on every stem.","p":28,"f":"n = 5 + 7 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 18"},{"q":"Referral ages have mean 26.9 and median 23.5, with a few referrals at 41, 44 and 52. What does the gap suggest?","a":"Positive skew. A mean well above the median is a quick sign that a long right tail of high values is pulling the mean upwards.","p":26},{"q":"Age at onset of psychosis in women peaks at 24 and 49 years; a report gives only the mean, 31. Why is this misleading?","a":"The distribution is bimodal, and the mean falls in the trough where few women actually have onset. Describe both peaks, or split the groups.","p":27},{"q":"Clinic A has 10 patients with mean score 20; clinic B has 30 with mean 40. What is the combined mean?","a":"35, not 30. Simply averaging two group means ignores group size; weight each mean by its number of patients.","p":24,"f":"Weighted mean = (10 × 20 + 30 × 40) / (10 + 30) = 1,400 / 40 = 35"},{"q":"Which average suits nominal data, and which suits log-normally distributed data?","a":"Nominal: the mode, the only option, since categories cannot be ordered or added. Log-normal: the geometric mean. Symmetrical data take the mean with SD; skewed or ordinal take the median with IQR.","p":24}]},{"t":"Range, IQR, variance and SD","s":29,"e":34,"c":[{"q":"A trial reports SD 12 for baseline scores in 36 patients. What is the standard error of the mean?","a":"SE = 2. Divide the SD by the square root of n, not by n: √36 = 6, so 12 ÷ 6 = 2. The SD describes patients; the SE describes how precisely the mean is pinned down.","p":30,"f":"SE = SD / √n"},{"q":"In a survey of 100 patients, 20% report side effects. What is the standard error of this proportion?","a":"0.04 (4 percentage points). Multiply 0.2 by 0.8 to get 0.16, divide by 100 to get 0.0016, then take the square root. Like any SE, it shrinks as the sample grows.","p":34,"f":"SE = √(p × (1 − p) / n)"},{"q":"A paper gives only the SE (1.5) for a mean, with n = 100. What is the SD?","a":"SD = 15. Reverse the SE formula: multiply the SE by √n, here 1.5 × 10. Useful when a paper hides the spread of patients behind a tidy-looking SE.","p":34,"f":"SD = SE × √n"},{"q":"A pilot of 50 patients gives an SE of 3.0. How many patients would halve the SE, if the SD is unchanged?","a":"About 200. The SE falls with √n, so halving it means doubling √n, which needs four times the patients. Doubling to 100 only divides the SE by √2, to about 2.1.","p":33,"f":"SE ∝ 1 / √n, so n × 4 halves SE"},{"q":"A box plot shows Q1 = 18 and Q3 = 30 days. What is the IQR, and what is the semi-interquartile range?","a":"IQR 12 days; semi-IQR 6. The IQR is the length of the box and holds the middle half of patients. The quartiles themselves are positions, not measures of spread, and the whiskers never enter the IQR.","p":32,"f":"IQR = Q3 − Q1"},{"q":"Three patients score 2, 4 and 6. Work out the sample variance and SD.","a":"Variance 4; SD 2. Deviations from the mean of 4 are −2, 0, 2; squares sum to 8. Divide by n − 1 (2), not n, or you underestimate. Variance is in squared units, so take the root.","p":33,"f":"s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / (n − 1); s = √s²"},{"q":"A trial doubles its sample size. What happens to the SD and to the SE of the mean?","a":"The SD stays roughly the same; the SE falls to about 71% (÷ √2). More patients do not make patients less variable, but they make the mean more precise.","p":30,"f":"SE = SD / √n"},{"q":"On an ogive, how does a centile differ from a percentile rank, and how do you read each?","a":"A centile is a score; a percentile rank is a percentage. For a centile, go across from the percentage to the curve, then down. For a rank, go up from the score, then across.","p":30},{"q":"Mean 40, SD 10, n = 100. Give the 95% CI for the mean and the 95% reference range for individuals.","a":"CI 38.0 to 42.0; reference range 20.4 to 59.6. The CI uses the SE (1), so it is narrow and locates the true mean. The reference range uses the SD and shows where most patients sit.","p":30,"f":"95% CI = mean ± 1.96 × SE; reference range = mean ± 1.96 × SD"},{"q":"Authors draw SE error bars to show how much patients varied in response. What is wrong?","a":"SE bars describe precision of the mean, not patient variability. They always look tighter than SD bars. Use the SD, or the IQR with the median if the data are skewed.","p":31}]},{"t":"Normal distribution, skewness, kurtosis","s":35,"e":39,"c":[{"q":"IQ is normal with mean 100 and SD 15. Roughly what percentage of people score above 130?","a":"About 2.5% (2.3% exactly). 130 is 2 SDs above the mean. 95% lie within 2 SDs, leaving 5% split between two tails, so halve it for one direction.","p":36,"f":"z = (x − mean) / SD"},{"q":"A T-score has mean 50 and SD 10. A patient scores 40. About what percentage score lower?","a":"About 16%. z = −1. Around 68% lie within 1 SD, leaving 32% in both tails, so 16% in the lower tail alone.","p":36,"f":"z = (40 − 50) / 10 = −1"},{"q":"For a normal distribution, what proportions lie within 1, 2 and 3 SDs of the mean, and what is the exact 95% multiplier?","a":"About 68%, 95% and 99.7%; exactly 95% lies within ±1.96 SD. The curve is symmetrical, with mean, median and mode equal, and is fully fixed by its mean and SD.","p":35},{"q":"Length of stay has a long tail of very long admissions. Name the skew and say how mean and median relate.","a":"Positive (right) skew: skew is named after the tail, not the peak. The long tail drags the mean above the median, and the 68-95-99.7 rule no longer applies.","p":36},{"q":"Two curves have the same SD, but one has a sharper peak and heavier tails. What is this called?","a":"Leptokurtic, with excess kurtosis above 0. Kurtosis is about tail weight, not spread. A flat-topped, light-tailed curve is platykurtic (excess kurtosis below 0); the normal has excess kurtosis 0.","p":39},{"q":"Admissions after self-harm average 3 per night, occurring independently. What proportion of nights have none?","a":"About 0.050, roughly 1 night in 20. For a Poisson count, the chance of zero events is e to the power minus the mean. The chance of at least one is 0.95.","p":37,"f":"P(0) = e^−λ = e^−3 ≈ 0.050"},{"q":"Weekly restraint incidents have a mean of 2 but a variance of 9. What does this suggest about a Poisson model?","a":"The counts are overdispersed. A Poisson distribution has its mean equal to its variance, so variance far above the mean means events cluster or rates vary, and the simple Poisson model fits poorly.","p":37},{"q":"You record how many of 50 sampled inpatients smoke, each with the same chance. Which distribution describes this count?","a":"Binomial: yes/no outcomes over a fixed number of trials with a constant probability. With large n it looks close to normal. Rare-event counts over time, with no fixed n, are Poisson instead.","p":39},{"q":"Right-skewed prolactin values are logged, averaged and antilogged. What is the result called, and where does it sit?","a":"The geometric mean. It sits close to the median and below the arithmetic mean, which the right tail pulls upward. Log transformation suits positively skewed, strictly positive data.","p":38,"f":"Geometric mean = antilog(mean of log x)"},{"q":"Which distribution is used for a mean from a small sample, and which for a statistic from a contingency table?","a":"Student's t for small-sample means: it has heavier tails than the normal, reflecting extra uncertainty. Chi-squared for statistics from tables such as a 2 × 4 table.","p":35}]},{"t":"Q-Q plots and normality tests","s":40,"e":45,"c":[{"q":"What is plotted on each axis of a normal Q-Q plot, and what pattern means normal data?","a":"Sorted observed values against the values a normal curve predicts for each rank. Normal data lie on a straight line; departures show first at the two ends.","p":41},{"q":"A Q-Q plot of CAMHS waits bends upward like a banana at both ends. What does it show, and what next?","a":"Positive (right) skew. Report median and IQR, analyse log-transformed waits, or use a rank-based test. Waits cannot fall below zero, so the short end is squashed and the long end stretches.","p":41},{"q":"On a Q-Q plot the lowest points fall below the line and the highest rise above it. Interpret.","a":"Heavy tails (leptokurtic). Both extremes are more extreme than a normal curve predicts. Skew bends both ends the same way; an S shape stretches them in opposite directions.","p":44},{"q":"A Q-Q plot flattens at both ends: low points above the line, high points below. What shape is this?","a":"Light tails (platykurtic), a reverse S. There are fewer extreme values than a normal curve predicts at both ends, as with values spread uniformly.","p":45},{"q":"MMSE scores in healthy volunteers produce a downward banana on a Q-Q plot. Name the shape and explain it.","a":"Negative (left) skew. Scores bunch against the ceiling, leaving a long tail at the low end. Squaring is a remedy for left skew; logging is for right skew.","p":44},{"q":"A Shapiro-Wilk test gives p = 0.01. State the null hypothesis and the conclusion.","a":"H₀: the data come from a normal distribution. p < 0.05 rejects it, so the data are not normal. Shapiro-Wilk, Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Anderson-Darling all share this null.","p":40},{"q":"With 20 patients, Shapiro-Wilk gives p = 0.40. The authors call the data normal. What is the flaw?","a":"A non-significant test is not proof of normality. With small samples the test has little power, so real skew can be missed. Look at the Q-Q plot or histogram.","p":42},{"q":"In a registry of 20,000 patients, a normality test gives p < 0.001, yet the Q-Q plot looks almost straight. Interpret.","a":"The departure is probably trivial. Huge samples make tests detect tiny deviations that do not matter. Trust the plot over the verdict.","p":42},{"q":"Before linear regression, an analyst checks the raw outcome for normality. What should be checked instead?","a":"The residuals. For regression the normality assumption is about the residuals; for t tests and ANOVA it applies within each group separately, not to the pooled raw data.","p":42},{"q":"Two groups' alcohol intakes are analysed on the natural log scale. What does the back-transformed difference in means represent?","a":"A ratio of geometric means, not a difference in units. Subtracting logs equals dividing the originals, so antilogging gives how many times higher one group's typical intake is.","p":43}]},{"t":"Confidence intervals","s":46,"e":51,"c":[{"q":"What does it mean to say a study's 95% confidence interval is 7.9 to 14.2?","a":"If the study were repeated many times, 95% of intervals built this way would contain the true value. The true mean is fixed; intervals vary. It is about the mean, not individual patients.","p":47},{"q":"A sample of 100 has mean 20 and SD 10. Calculate the 95% confidence interval for the mean.","a":"18.04 to 21.96. The SE is 10 ÷ 10 = 1, and 1.96 × 1 is added to and subtracted from the mean.","p":47,"f":"95% CI = mean ± 1.96 × SD / √n"},{"q":"Using the same data (mean 20, SE 1), work out the 99% CI. Is it wider or narrower?","a":"17.42 to 22.58: wider. More confidence needs a wider net, so the multiplier rises from 1.96 to 2.58.","p":47,"f":"99% CI = mean ± 2.58 × SE"},{"q":"A 95% CI from 25 patients is 6 points wide. How many patients would halve the width, with SD unchanged?","a":"About 100. Width depends on SE, which falls with √n, so four times the patients halves the width. Doubling to 50 narrows it only to about 71%.","p":49,"f":"Width ∝ 1 / √n"},{"q":"In 100 patients, 50% relapse. Give the approximate 95% CI for the proportion.","a":"About 40% to 60%. The SE is √(0.25 ÷ 100) = 0.05, and 1.96 × 0.05 ≈ 0.10 either side of 0.5.","p":47,"f":"95% CI = p ± 1.96 × √(p(1 − p) / n)"},{"q":"An odds ratio is 1.3 (95% CI 0.9 to 1.6). Is it significant at 5%? What is the null for an SMR?","a":"Not significant: the interval includes 1, the null for ratios. Differences use 0, and an SMR uses 100. A 95% CI excluding its null means p < 0.05.","p":48},{"q":"Two means differ by 3, each with SE 1, and their 95% CIs overlap. Is the difference significant?","a":"Yes, p ≈ 0.03. The SE of the difference is √2 ≈ 1.41, less than the sum of the SEs, so z ≈ 2.12. Overlapping CIs do not prove no difference.","p":50,"f":"SE(diff) = √(SE₁² + SE₂²); z = diff / SE(diff)"},{"q":"The smallest important difference is −4. A trial's 95% CI is −6.9 to −2.3. Interpret it.","a":"Significant, but it may or may not be clinically important. It excludes 0, yet straddles −4, so the true effect could fall on either side of the important threshold.","p":51},{"q":"A trial with 18 patients per arm reports a difference of −3.0 (95% CI −8.9 to 2.9). The authors say the drug does not work. Comment.","a":"Inconclusive, not negative. The CI crosses 0 but also includes large, important benefits. The trial is too small: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.","p":51},{"q":"With a small sample, what replaces 1.96 when building a CI for a mean? How are CIs for ratios built?","a":"A t value with n − 1 degrees of freedom, which is larger than 1.96. For ratios, work on the log scale, then exponentiate, which is why ratio CIs are asymmetrical.","p":47}]},{"t":"Histograms, bar charts, pie charts","s":52,"e":57,"c":[{"q":"One chart shows inpatients by diagnosis; another shows age at onset in 4-year bands. Name each chart type and why.","a":"Bar chart for diagnosis: nominal categories, separate bars with gaps, any order. Histogram for age: a continuous scale, so bars touch and area shows frequency.","p":53},{"q":"A histogram band from 20 to 40 years contains 100 patients. What should its height be if bins are unequal?","a":"5 patients per year of age. With unequal bins the y axis must be frequency density, so the bar's area, not its height, equals the count.","p":53,"f":"Frequency density = frequency / class width"},{"q":"An 18 to 25 band has density 24 per year; a 35 to 65 band has density 6. Which holds more patients?","a":"35 to 65: 6 × 30 = 180 patients versus 24 × 7 = 168. The tallest bar is not the biggest group when widths differ: area carries the count.","p":55,"f":"Count = frequency density × width"},{"q":"In a pie chart of 120 referrals, 30 come from GPs. What angle should the GP slice have?","a":"90°. The slice angle is the proportion times 360°, and 30 ÷ 120 = 0.25.","p":53,"f":"Angle = 360° × proportion"},{"q":"A poster uses a pie chart for mean waiting time in each of five teams. Why is this wrong?","a":"A pie shows parts of one whole. Mean waits for separate teams do not add up to a meaningful total. Pies also work badly with many small slices that are hard to compare.","p":54},{"q":"Stacked bars show diagnosis mix across three wards. Why is comparing the middle segments by eye unreliable?","a":"Only the bottom segment sits on a common baseline. Middle segments start at different heights, so their lengths are hard to compare. Stacked bars also hide differences in totals.","p":54},{"q":"On a cumulative frequency curve of AUDIT scores, how do you find the median and IQR?","a":"Go across from 50% to the curve, then down: that is the median. Do the same at 25% and 75% for the quartiles; their difference is the IQR. Misreading the gridline gives a quartile instead.","p":56},{"q":"In a frequency table, cumulative % is 40% at one episode and 62% at two. What is the median number of episodes?","a":"Two. The median lies in the first category where the cumulative % reaches 50%. The mode is simply the category with the highest frequency.","p":57},{"q":"Symptom severity is recorded as mild, moderate or severe. Should this be shown as a histogram?","a":"No: a bar chart. Ordinal data are still categorical, so bars stand apart, even though their order is now fixed by the categories.","p":54},{"q":"A bar chart's y axis starts at 80% rather than 0. What is the problem?","a":"A truncated axis exaggerates differences. Bar height should be proportional to the value, so small gaps look dramatic when the baseline is cut off.","p":57}]},{"t":"Box plots and stem-and-leaf plots","s":58,"e":63,"c":[{"q":"In a box plot with Tukey whiskers, what do the box, the line within it, the whiskers and the dots show?","a":"Box = Q1 to Q3 (IQR, middle 50%); line = median; whiskers reach the furthest value within 1.5 × IQR of the box; dots are outliers beyond. The box is not the range.","p":58},{"q":"Q1 = 10 and Q3 = 20. Using Tukey's rule, is a value of 38 an outlier?","a":"Yes. IQR = 10, so the upper fence is 20 + 15 = 35 (lower fence −5). 38 lies beyond it and is drawn as a dot. An outlier is not necessarily an error.","p":59,"f":"Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR"},{"q":"A box plot of untreated psychosis duration has the median near the bottom of the box and a long upper whisker. Interpret.","a":"Positive (right) skew: a long tail of very long waits. The mean will be pulled above the median, so report the median and IQR.","p":59},{"q":"One team's box is much longer than the others, with the median near its middle. Is it skewed?","a":"No: it is more spread, not skewed. Box length shows the IQR. Skew shows as lopsidedness, with unequal halves of the box and one long whisker.","p":60},{"q":"Must a box plot's whiskers always mark the minimum and maximum values?","a":"No. Many use Tukey's 1.5 × IQR rule and show more extreme values as dots; others run to min and max. Read the caption.","p":60},{"q":"A stem-and-leaf plot shows 5 | 3. What does it mean?","a":"It depends on the key: it could be 53, 5.3 or 530. Always read the key. Unlike a histogram, a stem-and-leaf keeps every original value.","p":60},{"q":"A stem-and-leaf plot holds 31 leaves. Which value is the median, and how do you find the mode?","a":"The 16th value in order, found by counting leaves along the stems. The mode is the most repeated leaf on a stem. The range runs from first to last leaf.","p":61,"f":"Median position = (n + 1) / 2"},{"q":"A dot plot shows screening scores by interview diagnosis with a cut-off line. Where are the false negatives?","a":"Dots in the 'has the condition' group lying below the cut-off: ill but screen negative. Dots above the line in the healthy group are false positives. Dot plots show overlap directly.","p":62},{"q":"What important features of the data can a box plot hide?","a":"Sample size and bimodality. Two peaks can collapse into one tidy box. A dot plot or histogram shows every patient and the true shape.","p":60},{"q":"Notched box plots for two groups have notches that do not overlap. What does this suggest?","a":"The medians probably differ, roughly equivalent to p < 0.05. Notches give an approximate interval around each median.","p":63}]},{"t":"Hypothesis testing, p values, type I/II errors","s":64,"e":69,"c":[{"q":"A trial reports p = 0.03. A colleague says this means there is a 3% chance the drug does not work. What is wrong?","a":"The direction is reversed. p is the probability of results at least this extreme assuming H₀ is true; it says nothing directly about how likely H₀ itself is.","p":64,"f":"p = P(data at least this extreme | H₀ true)"},{"q":"Define type I and type II errors, and give their usual conventional rates.","a":"Type I: false positive, rejecting a true H₀ (α, usually 0.05). Type II: false negative, missing a real effect (β, usually 0.2). Think 'I cry wolf', 'II miss the wolf'.","p":64},{"q":"A trial is designed with β = 0.2. What is its power, and what does that number mean?","a":"Power = 0.8. If the effect the trial was built to detect truly exists, there is an 80% chance the trial will reach significance.","p":64,"f":"power = 1 − β"},{"q":"On a plot of the test statistic under H₀ and H₁, which area represents β?","a":"The part of the H₁ curve lying inside the critical values. The effect is real, but the result falls short of significance, so it is missed.","p":65},{"q":"On the same plot, which areas make up α in a two-tailed test at 0.05?","a":"The two tails of the H₀ curve beyond ±1.96, 2.5% each. These are significant results arising when nothing is really going on.","p":65},{"q":"A trial concludes lithium augmentation prevents relapse (p = 0.02), but in truth it has no effect. Name this outcome.","a":"Type I error. The truth is 'no effect' (H₀ true), yet H₀ was rejected: a false positive, whose probability is α.","p":67},{"q":"Name four things that increase a study's power.","a":"Larger sample size, larger true effect, lower variance in the outcome and a higher α. Each pulls the H₀ and H₁ curves further apart or moves the threshold.","p":69},{"q":"Investigators want to detect an effect half the size originally planned, keeping power and α fixed. How must the sample size change?","a":"It must roughly quadruple. Required n scales with 1 / effect², so halving the effect gives 1 / (½)² = 4 times the patients.","p":64,"f":"n ∝ 1 / (effect size)²"},{"q":"From a power curve, about how many patients per arm give 80% power for Cohen's d = 0.5 at α = 0.05, two-tailed?","a":"About 64 per arm, 128 in total. A large effect (d = 0.8) needs only about 26 per arm; a small one (d = 0.3) about 176.","p":68},{"q":"A small pilot trial finds p = 0.21 and the authors conclude the intervention has no effect. Criticise this.","a":"Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. An underpowered study may simply have missed a real effect (type II error). Look at the confidence interval for plausible effects.","p":66}]},{"t":"Frequentist and Bayesian testing","s":70,"e":74,"c":[{"q":"Contrast what a frequentist p value and a Bayesian posterior each give you.","a":"A p value gives P(data | H₀): how surprising the data are if nothing works. A posterior gives P(hypothesis | data): how likely the effect is, given the data and prior.","p":70},{"q":"How is a Bayesian posterior obtained from the prior and the data?","a":"Multiply the prior by the likelihood (what the trial data say). The posterior sits between them, pulled towards whichever is more precise.","p":71,"f":"posterior ∝ prior × likelihood"},{"q":"Interpret a 95% credible interval of 0.8 to 4.7 PHQ-9 points.","a":"Given the prior and the data, there is a 95% probability the true reduction lies between 0.8 and 4.7 points. It is a direct probability statement about the parameter.","p":70},{"q":"What does a frequentist 95% confidence interval actually mean?","a":"It describes the method: if the study were repeated many times, 95% of intervals built this way would contain the true value. It is not a 95% probability for this one interval.","p":70},{"q":"A trial estimates 2.8 points with SE 1.0. What is the z statistic, and roughly what two-tailed p value follows?","a":"z = 2.8, giving p ≈ 0.005. The estimate lies 2.8 standard errors from zero, well beyond 1.96.","p":71,"f":"z = estimate / SE"},{"q":"Identical trial data give P(effect > 2 points) of 20%, 78% or 90%. What explains the difference, and what should a good report include?","a":"Different priors: sceptical, vague and enthusiastic. Because the posterior depends on the prior, a good Bayesian report shows sensitivity analyses across plausible priors.","p":72},{"q":"When does the prior matter most in a Bayesian analysis?","a":"When the data are imprecise, such as a small trial. Weak data barely move the posterior away from the prior; large precise data swamp it.","p":74},{"q":"Prior density at zero is 0.399 and posterior density at zero is 0.079. Calculate BF₁₀ and interpret it.","a":"BF₁₀ ≈ 5.0: the data are about five times more likely under an effect than under none, moderate evidence for H₁ (Savage-Dickey ratio).","p":73,"f":"BF₁₀ = prior height at 0 / posterior height at 0 = 0.399 / 0.079"},{"q":"A mindfulness trial reports BF₀₁ = 14. What does this mean?","a":"Strong evidence for no effect. BF₀₁ puts H₀ on top, so BF₁₀ = 1 / 14 ≈ 0.07: the data are 14 times more likely under H₀. Always check the subscript order.","p":74,"f":"BF₁₀ = 1 / BF₀₁"},{"q":"A Bayes factor of 1 is reported. A colleague calls this evidence of no effect. Is that right?","a":"No. BF = 1 means the data are equally likely under both hypotheses, so they favour neither. Evidence for H₀ needs BF₁₀ well below 1 (for example below 1/3).","p":71}]},{"t":"Test assumptions and Levene's test","s":75,"e":79,"c":[{"q":"What three assumptions do parametric tests such as the t test and ANOVA make?","a":"Independent observations, approximately normal data (or residuals), and equal variances across groups, called homoscedasticity.","p":75},{"q":"What is the null hypothesis of Levene's test?","a":"That the groups have equal variances. It examines spread only, never the means.","p":75,"f":"H₀: σ₁² = σ₂² (= σ₃² ...)"},{"q":"Levene's test gives p < 0.001 before comparing two groups' means. What does this mean and which test follows?","a":"Variances are unequal, so do not pool the SDs. Use Welch's t test, which estimates each group's variance separately.","p":76},{"q":"Group SDs are 4.6 and 14.3. Roughly how many times larger is one variance than the other?","a":"About 10 times. Variance is SD squared: 204.5 / 21.2 ≈ 9.7. A threefold SD gap becomes a tenfold variance gap.","p":76,"f":"variance ratio = 14.3² / 4.6²"},{"q":"Levene's p = 0.76 and the data look roughly normal. Which test compares two independent means?","a":"Student's (pooled) t test. p ≥ 0.05 gives no evidence of unequal variances. Many statisticians use Welch by default as it costs little, but the classic answer is Student's t.","p":78},{"q":"A residuals versus fitted plot fans out into a funnel. Which assumption fails, and what are the remedies?","a":"Homoscedasticity. Transform the outcome (for example log), use robust (sandwich) standard errors or weighted least squares.","p":77},{"q":"Does heteroscedasticity bias regression coefficients?","a":"No. The coefficients remain unbiased; it is their standard errors, confidence intervals and p values that become unreliable.","p":77},{"q":"You want a formal normality test for sleep-onset times in 30 patients. Which test, and why not Kolmogorov-Smirnov?","a":"Shapiro-Wilk, the most powerful common normality test in small samples. Kolmogorov-Smirnov is weaker and better suited to large samples or a fully specified distribution.","p":79},{"q":"What is the Brown-Forsythe test, and when is it preferred?","a":"Levene's test using the median rather than the mean. It is more robust when the data are skewed.","p":76},{"q":"Variances are unequal across three groups. What replaces one-way ANOVA, and which post hoc test fits?","a":"Welch ANOVA, followed by Games-Howell post hoc comparisons, both of which allow for unequal variances.","p":76}]},{"t":"Comparing means: t tests and ANOVA","s":80,"e":85,"c":[{"q":"Which test compares mean HAM-D in the same patients before and after treatment, and what is the common mistake?","a":"Paired t test. Using an unpaired t test ignores the pairing, wastes the within-person correlation and loses power.","p":82},{"q":"A three-arm trial gives one-way ANOVA F = 10.5, p < 0.001. What can you conclude, and what next?","a":"At least one group mean differs, but not which. Run post hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD to compare pairs while controlling family-wise error.","p":81},{"q":"Three unadjusted t tests are run when no true differences exist. What is the chance of at least one false positive?","a":"About 14%, almost three times the intended 5%. This is why ANOVA plus adjusted post hoc tests is preferred.","p":81,"f":"P(≥ 1 false positive) = 1 − 0.95³ = 0.14"},{"q":"Using a Bonferroni correction for three pairwise comparisons, what significance threshold applies to each?","a":"About 0.017. Dividing α by the number of tests keeps the overall type I error at roughly 5%, at some cost to power.","p":80,"f":"α per test = 0.05 / 3 ≈ 0.017"},{"q":"What does the ANOVA F ratio compare?","a":"Variation between group means against variation within groups. A large F means the groups differ more than chance scatter would predict.","p":81,"f":"F = between-group variance / within-group variance"},{"q":"Why analyse a trial with ANCOVA using baseline score as a covariate?","a":"To adjust for baseline severity. Removing variation explained by baseline usually narrows confidence intervals and increases precision.","p":83},{"q":"A colleague describes ANCOVA as 'ANOVA with two outcomes'. Correct them.","a":"ANCOVA adds a continuous covariate to ANOVA. Two or more outcomes analysed together is MANOVA; both together is MANCOVA.","p":82},{"q":"In a 2 × 2 factorial trial, sertraline lowers HAM-D by 4.2 points without CBT and 8.5 with CBT (interaction p = 0.02). Interpret.","a":"A significant interaction: sertraline's benefit depends on whether CBT is given. Report each drug's effect at each level of the other, not just main effects.","p":84},{"q":"Mean IQ in 40 adults with a 22q11 deletion is compared with the population mean of 100. Which test?","a":"One-sample t test: a single sample compared against a known fixed value.","p":84},{"q":"Three drugs are compared on BPRS at baseline, 6 and 12 weeks, asking whether groups change at different rates. Which analysis?","a":"Mixed (between × within) ANOVA. Drug is between-subjects, time within-subjects; the drug × time interaction answers the question.","p":84}]},{"t":"Non-parametric tests","s":86,"e":90,"c":[{"q":"Give the non-parametric equivalents of the unpaired t test, paired t test, one-way ANOVA, repeated-measures ANOVA and Pearson correlation.","a":"Mann-Whitney U, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal-Wallis, Friedman and Spearman's rank correlation respectively.","p":86},{"q":"Length of admission on two wards is strongly right-skewed. Which test compares them, and how should results be summarised?","a":"Mann-Whitney U (Wilcoxon rank-sum) test. Report medians and IQRs, since a few very long stays drag the mean.","p":87},{"q":"A paper uses the Wilcoxon signed-rank test to compare two independent groups. What is wrong?","a":"Signed-rank is for paired data, ranking within-pair differences. Independent groups need the rank-sum test, i.e. Mann-Whitney U.","p":87},{"q":"Why are rank-based tests less affected by outliers?","a":"Values are replaced by ranks, so an extreme value simply takes the top rank and loses its pull, however far out it lies.","p":87},{"q":"Ten patients' sleep changes are ranked by absolute size; the two negative changes have ranks 2 and 5. What is Wilcoxon's T?","a":"T = 7, the smaller signed rank sum. All ranks total 55, so positive ranks sum to 48.","p":88,"f":"total ranks = n(n + 1) / 2 = 10 × 11 / 2 = 55; T = 2 + 5 = 7"},{"q":"Two clinics have identical median waits, yet Mann-Whitney gives p = 0.004. How can this be?","a":"Mann-Whitney compares whole distributions via ranks, not medians. One clinic's long upper tail takes more high ranks. It tests medians only when distributions share the same shape.","p":89},{"q":"Does 'non-parametric' mean a test has no assumptions?","a":"No. Observations must still be independent or correctly paired. Non-parametric tests only drop the assumption about distribution shape.","p":87},{"q":"Agitation is measured in the same patients on three or more occasions with skewed data. Which test, and which is commonly misused instead?","a":"Friedman test. Kruskal-Wallis is wrongly chosen, but it assumes independent groups, not repeated measures in the same people.","p":87},{"q":"Patients report only whether sleep improved or worsened after switching antipsychotic; size of change is unknown. Which test?","a":"Sign test: a binomial test on the number of improvements, using direction alone when the magnitude of paired change is unavailable.","p":90},{"q":"What is the cost of using a rank test when data really are normal?","a":"A modest loss of power, because ranking discards some information in the actual values.","p":86}]},{"t":"Categorical tests: chi-squared, Fisher","s":91,"e":95,"c":[{"q":"120 patients per arm; 82 of 240 are readmitted overall. Under no association, what is the expected number readmitted in the outreach arm?","a":"41. Expected counts use only the margins; with equal arms, each would hold half the 82 readmissions.","p":91,"f":"E = row total × column total / grand total = 120 × 82 / 240 = 41"},{"q":"Give the chi-squared statistic formula and explain what makes it large.","a":"Sum, over every cell, of the squared gap between observed and expected, divided by expected. Big gaps between what was seen and what no association predicts inflate χ².","p":91,"f":"χ² = Σ (O − E)² / E"},{"q":"How many degrees of freedom does a chi-squared test on a 3 × 4 contingency table have?","a":"6. Once the margins are fixed, only that many cells can vary freely.","p":91,"f":"df = (rows − 1) × (columns − 1) = 2 × 3 = 6"},{"q":"When should Fisher's exact test replace chi-squared, and what is the commonest error in applying the rule?","a":"When any expected count is below 5. The trap is checking observed counts instead; the rule applies to expected counts.","p":92},{"q":"What does Yates' continuity correction do, and when is it used?","a":"It subtracts 0.5 from each |O − E| before squaring, in 2 × 2 tables only. This shrinks χ², making the test more conservative.","p":92},{"q":"Anxiety (yes/no) is measured in the same 150 patients before and after a group intervention. Which test, and which cells does it use?","a":"McNemar's test, using only the discordant pairs. Patients unchanged tell you nothing about change.","p":93},{"q":"In paired anxiety data, 25 patients lose anxiety and 9 develop it. Calculate McNemar's χ² with continuity correction.","a":"χ² ≈ 6.62, p ≈ 0.01: anxiety became significantly less common.","p":93,"f":"χ² = (|b − c| − 1)² / (b + c) = (16 − 1)² / 34 = 225 / 34 ≈ 6.62"},{"q":"χ² = 5.2 with 2 degrees of freedom. Is this significant at 5%?","a":"No. The 2 df critical value is 5.99, so p ≈ 0.07. With 1 df (critical value 3.84) the same χ² would be significant. More df demand a larger χ².","p":94},{"q":"Schizophrenia births across the four seasons are compared with equal expected numbers. Which test, and how many df?","a":"Chi-squared goodness-of-fit test, with 3 df: one categorical variable checked against an expected distribution.","p":95,"f":"df = categories − 1 = 4 − 1 = 3"},{"q":"Depression (yes/no) is compared between rural and urban adults in a survey of 8,000. Which test?","a":"Chi-squared test of association: two independent groups, a binary outcome and large expected counts, so no need for Fisher.","p":95}]},{"t":"Survival analysis: Kaplan-Meier","s":96,"e":101,"c":[{"q":"On a Kaplan-Meier curve of time to relapse, what does the y axis show, and what is the commonest misreading?","a":"Proportion still relapse-free. Everyone starts at 1.0 and the curve only falls. The trap is reading it as the proportion who have relapsed; that is one minus the curve value.","p":98},{"q":"On a Kaplan-Meier plot, what is the difference between a step down and a small vertical tick?","a":"Step = event; tick = censored person. A censored patient was still event-free when last seen (joined late or left), so they leave the risk set on a flat part of the curve without causing a drop.","p":97},{"q":"At 12 months a maintenance arm's curve reads 0.63. What percentage have relapsed by then?","a":"37%. The curve gives the share still well (63%), so relapsed is the complement.","p":98,"f":"Relapsed by t = 1 − S(t)"},{"q":"How do you read median time to relapse from a Kaplan-Meier curve?","a":"Go across from 0.5 on the y axis to the curve, then drop down to the time axis. It is the first time at which half the group have had the event.","p":98,"f":"Median = first t where S(t) ≤ 0.5"},{"q":"A self-harm cohort's Kaplan-Meier curve ends at 0.64 after 18 months of follow-up. What is the median time to repeat self-harm?","a":"Not reached. Half the cohort never had the event, so no median exists. Quoting 18 months, the end of the axis, is the classic error; give the percentage event-free at a fixed time instead.","p":99},{"q":"Why use Kaplan-Meier rather than simply comparing crude relapse counts such as 67/140 against 102/140?","a":"Follow-up is uneven. A crude count treats someone seen for 15 months like someone seen for 24. Kaplan-Meier uses each person's time at risk for as long as it lasted, and copes with censoring.","p":98,"f":"S(t) = Π (1 − dᵢ ÷ nᵢ)"},{"q":"What does the log-rank test compare, and what does it not give you?","a":"Whole survival curves. It sums observed minus expected events at every event time. It yields a p value only: no effect size and no adjustment for covariates.","p":98,"f":"χ² = Σ(O − E)² ÷ V, 1 df for two groups"},{"q":"A Cox model gives a hazard ratio of 0.49 (95% CI 0.36 to 0.67) for maintenance versus discontinuation. Interpret it.","a":"Maintenance roughly halves the relapse rate at any moment, and the CI excludes 1, so it is significant. It does not mean 51% of relapses were prevented by the end of follow-up.","p":98,"f":"HR = hazard₁ ÷ hazard₀"},{"q":"Two Kaplan-Meier curves cross at 6 months; log-rank p = 0.23 and Cox HR = 1.18. What is the problem?","a":"Proportional hazards fails. Early benefit and later harm cancel out, so the log-rank test misses a real difference and one hazard ratio describes neither period. Report the curves, period-specific HRs or restricted mean survival time.","p":100},{"q":"Why are estimates at the right-hand end of a Kaplan-Meier curve the least trustworthy, and what assumption about censoring underpins every method?","a":"Few remain at risk, so each event moves the curve a lot. All Kaplan-Meier methods assume non-informative censoring: those censored have the same outlook as those still followed.","p":98}]},{"t":"Multiple testing: Bonferroni","s":102,"e":107,"c":[{"q":"Ten independent tests are run at α = 0.05 and every null hypothesis is true. What is the chance of at least one false positive?","a":"About 40%. The chance that all ten are correctly non-significant is 0.95¹⁰ ≈ 0.60; the rest is the family-wise error rate.","p":104,"f":"FWER = 1 − (1 − α)ᵏ = 1 − 0.95¹⁰ ≈ 0.40"},{"q":"Five true-null independent tests at α = 0.05: why is 25% the wrong answer for the chance of at least one false positive?","a":"The answer is 23%. Adding 5% per test (k × α) overstates the risk and passes 100% beyond 20 tests. Calculate the chance of no false positives and subtract from 1.","p":105,"f":"1 − 0.95⁵ = 1 − 0.774 = 0.226"},{"q":"A trial reports 10 secondary outcomes with p values 0.001, 0.004, 0.006, 0.012, 0.021, 0.038 and four above 0.05. How many survive Bonferroni?","a":"Two. The per-test threshold is 0.05 ÷ 10 = 0.005, so only 0.001 and 0.004 remain significant.","p":103,"f":"Bonferroni threshold = α ÷ k"},{"q":"With the same ten p values, Holm's procedure keeps three results. Why does it beat Bonferroni here?","a":"Holm steps the threshold up after each rejection: 0.001 < 0.005, 0.004 < 0.0056, 0.006 < 0.00625, but 0.012 > 0.0071, so stop. It controls family-wise error equally and never rejects fewer.","p":104,"f":"i-th smallest p vs α ÷ (k − i + 1)"},{"q":"Benjamini-Hochberg applied to the ten outcomes keeps five. What does it control that Bonferroni does not?","a":"The false discovery rate: the expected proportion of false positives among results declared significant. Bonferroni and Holm control the chance of any false positive at all, so they are stricter.","p":104,"f":"Largest i with p₍ᵢ₎ ≤ i × q ÷ k"},{"q":"Body weight, BMI and waist circumference are all tested as separate outcomes. Why does Bonferroni become overly harsh?","a":"The outcomes are highly correlated, so there are fewer truly independent chances of error than tests. Bonferroni then overcorrects, losing power and raising the type II error rate.","p":104},{"q":"Why is the genome-wide significance threshold set at p < 5 × 10⁻⁸?","a":"It is roughly a Bonferroni correction for about one million independent common variants across the genome.","p":106,"f":"0.05 ÷ 10⁶ = 5 × 10⁻⁸"},{"q":"On a Manhattan plot, eight dots lie above the genome-wide line, clustered in four peaks. How many loci are significant?","a":"Four. Each peak is a cluster of correlated variants at one locus, so count peaks, not dots.","p":106},{"q":"A significant one-way ANOVA compares four antidepressants. Which post hoc approach suits all pairwise comparisons, and which suits three doses each versus one placebo?","a":"Tukey's honestly significant difference for all pairs (Games-Howell if variances differ). Dunnett's test for several treatments against a single control; it is more powerful than Bonferroni for that design.","p":106},{"q":"A trial finds benefit only in a post hoc subgroup of 'responders'. What is needed before believing it, and when is no correction needed at all?","a":"A pre-specified hypothesis and a formal test of interaction. A single pre-specified primary outcome needs no multiplicity correction; repeated interim looks need alpha spending, such as O'Brien-Fleming.","p":104}]},{"t":"Choosing the right statistical test","s":108,"e":113,"c":[{"q":"What three questions pick almost every statistical test for comparing groups?","a":"Outcome type (normal continuous, ordinal or skewed, categorical, time to event); how many groups; and whether they are independent or paired. Then ask about extras such as covariates.","p":108},{"q":"24 adolescents with anorexia have BMI measured before and after treatment; the differences are skewed (Shapiro-Wilk p = 0.017). Which test?","a":"Wilcoxon signed-rank. Same people twice makes it paired; skewed differences rule out the paired t test, so move to its rank-based twin.","p":109},{"q":"For a paired t test, which data must be roughly normal?","a":"The within-person differences, not the raw before and after scores. The paired t test is a one-sample t test on the differences.","p":110},{"q":"Change in HAM-D in 45 CBT and 45 counselling patients is roughly normal with similar SDs. Which test, and why is ANOVA with Tukey pointless here?","a":"Unpaired t test. A one-way ANOVA on two groups gives the same p value because F = t², and post hoc tests mean nothing with only one pair.","p":111,"f":"F = t² (two groups)"},{"q":"Give the rank-based twin of the unpaired t test, one-way ANOVA and repeated-measures ANOVA.","a":"Mann-Whitney U (Wilcoxon rank-sum), Kruskal-Wallis, and Friedman respectively. Using Kruskal-Wallis for the same people measured three times is a classic error.","p":109},{"q":"Readmission (yes/no) is compared between two clinics, but one cell has an expected count of 3. Which test, and what if the yes/no data were paired?","a":"Fisher's exact test, because chi-squared is unreliable when any expected count is under 5. Paired yes/no data need McNemar's test; three or more paired occasions need Cochran's Q.","p":109},{"q":"A mixed-design ANOVA compares PANSS over three time points in three drug arms. Which term shows whether the arms improve at different rates?","a":"The drug × time interaction. Different rates means non-parallel lines. The drug main effect averages over all time points, including baseline when groups should match.","p":112},{"q":"Name the extension of ANOVA for adjusting for a baseline covariate, for two factors, and for two or more outcomes analysed together.","a":"ANCOVA for a covariate such as baseline score; two-way ANOVA for two factors such as drug and sex; MANOVA for several outcomes at once.","p":109},{"q":"For prediction, which regression model suits a continuous, a binary, a count and a censored time-to-event outcome?","a":"Linear, logistic (odds ratios), Poisson or negative binomial (rate ratios), and Cox (hazard ratios). The type of outcome chooses the model; clustered or repeated data need a mixed model or GEE.","p":112},{"q":"A quick cognitive test correlates r = 0.9 with the MMSE. Why does this not show the two agree, and what should be used?","a":"Correlation shows the scores move together, not that they match; one could be consistently higher. Use a Bland-Altman plot of differences against means. For two raters on categories use Cohen's kappa.","p":113}]},{"t":"Correlation: Pearson, Spearman","s":114,"e":119,"c":[{"q":"Sleep and PHQ-9 correlate with r = −0.63. What share of variation do they have in common?","a":"About 40%. Square r to get the shared variance. Reading r itself as 63% is the trap; the remaining 60% comes from other factors.","p":115,"f":"r² = 0.63 × 0.63 ≈ 0.40"},{"q":"Is r = −0.8 weaker than r = +0.5?","a":"No, it is stronger. The sign gives direction only; the size gives strength. As a rough guide, |r| under 0.3 is weak, 0.3 to 0.7 moderate, over 0.7 strong.","p":116},{"q":"How do the quadrants around the two means of a scatter plot determine the sign of Pearson's r?","a":"Each point contributes the product of its deviations from both means. Top-right and bottom-left give positive products; top-left and bottom-right negative. Whichever dominates sets the sign.","p":115,"f":"r = covariance ÷ (SDx × SDy)"},{"q":"Why is r = −0.63 not the same as 'each extra hour of sleep lowers PHQ-9 by 0.63 points'?","a":"r has no units and is not a slope. The regression slope here is −2.69 points per hour. Change sleep to minutes and r is unchanged, but the slope changes.","p":116,"f":"b = r × SDy ÷ SDx"},{"q":"A scatter plot shows a clear inverted U between arousal and performance, yet r ≈ 0. What does that mean?","a":"A strong relationship that is not linear. Pearson's r only measures straight-line association, so r = 0 does not mean no relationship. Always plot the data.","p":116},{"q":"AUDIT score and days of sick leave are both strongly right-skewed with extreme values. Which correlation coefficient fits?","a":"Spearman's rank correlation (or Kendall's tau, better for small samples with many ties). Ranking tames skew and outliers and captures any monotonic relationship.","p":117},{"q":"ADHD and substance use in 240 adolescents: a = 26, b = 34, c = 22, d = 158. Calculate the phi coefficient.","a":"φ ≈ 0.34. ad − bc = 4108 − 748 = 3360; √(60 × 180 × 48 × 192) ≈ 9977. Check: φ² = χ² ÷ n = 27.22 ÷ 240 ≈ 0.11.","p":118,"f":"φ = (ad − bc) ÷ √(row₁ × row₂ × col₁ × col₂)"},{"q":"Self-harm in the past year (yes/no) is correlated with a continuous impulsivity score. Which coefficient, and what if the binary variable were a cut-off on a continuum?","a":"Point-biserial for a genuinely binary variable; it equals Pearson's r with 0/1 coding. If the dichotomy cuts an underlying continuum, use biserial correlation.","p":117},{"q":"What does partial correlation do, and how does canonical correlation differ?","a":"Partial correlation measures the link between two variables while holding a third constant. Canonical correlation relates two sets of variables to each other.","p":117},{"q":"A cross-sectional study finds short sleep correlates with higher depression scores. Name two reasons this cannot show sleep causes depression.","a":"Reverse causation: depression disturbs sleep. Confounding: a shared cause may drive both. Correlation also depends on sample range and size.","p":116}]},{"t":"Simple linear regression","s":120,"e":125,"c":[{"q":"A regression gives intercept 1.79 and slope 0.49 for fall in PHQ-9 per CBT session. Predict the fall for 12 sessions.","a":"7.67 points. Twelve sessions add 5.88; the intercept is added once. Forgetting the intercept (5.88) is the commonest error.","p":121,"f":"ŷ = a + bx = 1.79 + 0.49 × 12 = 7.67"},{"q":"What do the slope and the intercept of a simple linear regression mean, and which carries units?","a":"Slope: average change in y per one-unit increase in x, in y-units per x-unit. Intercept: predicted y when x = 0. Both carry y's units; r has none.","p":120},{"q":"A patient attended 9 sessions and their PHQ-9 fell by 14 points, using ŷ = 1.79 + 0.49x. What is the residual?","a":"+7.8. Predicted fall = 1.79 + 4.41 = 6.2, so observed minus predicted is 14 − 6.2. Positive means they did better than the line predicted.","p":122,"f":"Residual = y − ŷ"},{"q":"What does 'least squares' minimise in linear regression?","a":"The sum of squared vertical distances (residuals) between each observed point and the fitted line. Squaring stops positive and negative residuals cancelling.","p":121,"f":"Minimise Σ(y − ŷ)²"},{"q":"In a simple linear regression with r = 0.67, what is R² and what does it mean?","a":"About 0.45. In simple regression R² = r²: the line explains about 45% of the variation in the outcome.","p":121,"f":"R² = regression SS ÷ total SS = r²"},{"q":"At 12 sessions the 95% confidence interval is ±0.8 points but the prediction interval is ±6.1. Why the difference?","a":"The confidence band describes the mean line, which is estimated precisely. The prediction band covers one new individual, who also scatters around the line, so it is much wider.","p":121},{"q":"The model was fitted on patients attending 1 to 20 sessions. Why is predicting the outcome for 40 sessions unwise?","a":"Extrapolation. Nothing shows the straight-line relationship holds outside the observed range of x; it may flatten or reverse.","p":122},{"q":"A patient with far more sessions than anyone else improved little, and the slope drops from 0.49 to 0.34 when included. Describe this point.","a":"High leverage and influential. An extreme x gives leverage; sitting off the line makes it pull the slope. Cook's distance combines both. Check the data and report results with and without it.","p":123},{"q":"A residual-versus-fitted plot shows a U shape. What does it mean and what is the fix?","a":"Non-linearity: a straight line is the wrong shape. Add a squared term (polynomial regression) or transform x.","p":124},{"q":"A residual plot fans out as fitted values rise. Name the problem and its consequence.","a":"Heteroscedasticity. Coefficients stay unbiased but standard errors and p values are wrong. Use a log transform or robust standard errors. Normality of residuals is checked separately on a Q-Q plot.","p":125}]},{"t":"Multiple, multilevel and multivariate regression","s":126,"e":134,"c":[{"q":"In a multiple regression of weight change, olanzapine (vs aripiprazole) has B = 4.65 kg. Interpret it.","a":"On average 4.65 kg more gain than aripiprazole in people of the same age, baseline BMI and sex. It is adjusted and compares only with the reference drug.","p":127},{"q":"Drug has three categories (aripiprazole, olanzapine, risperidone). How does it enter a regression, and why not as 1, 2, 3?","a":"As k − 1 = 2 dummy variables against a reference (aripiprazole). Coding 1, 2, 3 wrongly forces equal ordered steps onto a nominal variable.","p":128,"f":"Dummies = k − 1"},{"q":"Olanzapine B = 4.65 and risperidone B = 1.66, both versus aripiprazole. Estimate the olanzapine versus risperidone difference.","a":"About 3 kg. Both share the same reference, so subtract the coefficients (the text quotes 2.98 from unrounded values).","p":128,"f":"b(olz) − b(ris) = 4.65 − 1.66 ≈ 3.0 kg"},{"q":"Age has B = −0.14 kg per year in the weight model. Predict the difference in weight change between two otherwise identical patients aged 30 and 50.","a":"2.8 kg less gain at age 50, holding drug, baseline BMI and sex constant. Continuous coefficients are per unit, so multiply by the 20-year difference.","p":128,"f":"−0.14 × 20 = −2.8 kg"},{"q":"Units per week and log GGT are each non-significant, yet the whole model is highly significant and their VIFs are 19.2. Explain.","a":"Multicollinearity. The two predictors carry nearly the same information (r = 0.97), so standard errors inflate and neither gets credit. Drop one, combine them or report a joint test.","p":129},{"q":"A predictor has VIF 19.2. What proportion of its variance is explained by the other predictors?","a":"About 95%. The coefficient's variance is inflated roughly 19-fold. VIF above 5 to 10 suggests a collinearity problem; 1 means no overlap.","p":129,"f":"VIF = 1 ÷ (1 − R²ⱼ) → R²ⱼ = 1 − 1 ÷ 19.2 ≈ 0.95"},{"q":"An age × olanzapine interaction term is −0.12 (p = 0.003). What does it mean?","a":"The effect of olanzapine on weight depends on age: it is largest in younger patients. The slopes against age differ (−0.23 vs −0.11 kg/year), so the lines are not parallel.","p":130},{"q":"How does effect modification (interaction) differ from confounding?","a":"Interaction means an effect truly varies across levels of another variable. Confounding distorts the overall estimate of an effect through a third variable linked to both exposure and outcome.","p":130},{"q":"A model has R² = 0.56 and adjusted R² = 0.55. Why report adjusted R²?","a":"R² can only rise as predictors are added, even useless ones. Adjusted R² penalises extra predictors, guarding against overfitting.","p":126},{"q":"What is the difference between multivariable, multilevel and multivariate regression?","a":"Multivariable: many predictors, one outcome. Multilevel: clustered or repeated data, such as patients within wards. Multivariate: several outcomes modelled together.","p":128}]}];
